{"id":"d4281e4a-70cc-4620-97e3-2fea484b64ca","object":"chat.completion","created":1793331347,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Checking imports at the top of `environ.py` and whether `logger.py` already imports the config module (to avoid a circular import).\n\n```python\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", 1, 30)[\"content\"])\nprint(\"=====\")\nprint(search_code(\"environ\", [\"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\"], 10))\n```","reasoning_content":"I have already registered full reads of all three files and baseline pytest done. Phase=edit. Now I need to implement the changes.\n\nLet me recall the environ.py structure: I need to add an EnvVar factory method for max message chars, or just add a descriptor in envs. The requirement: new config item `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` in envs. Unset -> None. Set -> int parse, must be >= 32, ValueError containing variable name. Caching via descriptor already wraps error with name prefix.\n\nThe descriptor wraps exceptions with f\"{self.name}: {e}\". But the requirement says error text must contain variable name. That's satisfied by the wrapper. But careful: raising ValueError inside init, the wrapper raises type(e)(f\"{name}: {e}\") — good.\n\nHowever note `EnvVar.int_or_none()` returns int(v) without validation, and error message wrapped with name. I can add a factory method `int_or_none_min` or use `EnvVar(lambda v: ...)` in the class body. Let's add a static factory method `int_or_none_with_min(*, minimum: int)` — \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法\". Let's implement:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nWait: empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError -> wrapped. Good.\n\nThen in envs Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\nNow logger.py. Need to modify NewLineFormatter:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n        ...\n```\n\nThen in format():\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        # 先完成参数替换\n        record.message = record.getMessage()\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            record.message = _truncate_message(record.message, self.max_message_chars)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n```\n\nBut careful: logging.Formatter.format calls record.getMessage() then sets record.message, then formats using record.message. If we pre-set record.message before calling super().format, super will recompute record.message = record.getMessage() overwriting truncation. Hmm. Actually logging.Formatter.format:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.usesTime():\n        record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n    s = self.formatMessage(record)\n    if record.exc_info:\n        ...\n```\n\nSo record.message is recomputed. Options: temporarily set record.msg = truncated and record.args = None, then restore after. Requirement: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info