r: temporarily replace record.getMessage? Can't set attribute on instance? You can — record.getMessage = lambda: truncated? LogRecord instances allow attribute assignment (no __slots__). Actually logging.LogRecord doesn't define __slots__, so yes. But super().format calls self.formatMessage(record) which uses record.message. So set record.message after getMessage... it's overwritten. Setting record.getMessage lambda is hacky too.\n\nBetter: implement our own formatting that mirrors base but with truncated message:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    full_message = record.getMessage()\n    displayed = full_message\n    limit = self.max_message_chars\n    if limit is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        displayed = _truncate_message(full_message, limit)\n    record.message = displayed\n    ...\n```\n\nStill need to avoid super's getMessage. Approach: override getMessage via temporary swap as said. Hmm, maybe cleanest: compute record.message before, then don't call super().format(record) but replicate:\n\nActually we can do:\n\n```python\nsaved = record.msg, record.args\ntry:\n    if truncation needed:\n        record.msg = truncated\n        record.args = None\n    msg = super().format(record)\nfinally:\n    record.msg, record.args = saved\n    record.message = full_message  # restore\n```\n\nWait for no-truncation case, super sets record.message = full message (same as original). Fine.\n\nBut subtle: multi-line prefix logic uses `record.message` and msg.find(record.message): after super().format with truncated msg, record.message is truncated message, and msg contains it. Good. For no truncation, unchanged.\n\nAlso exception text: exc_info appended by super().format, not affected by msg truncation. Good.\n\nEdge: when truncation with args and fmt uses %(message)s, fine.\n\nBut also careful: `record.args` may be a dict; restore as saved.\n\nNow `_truncate_message`:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, limit: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= limit:\n        return message\n    remaining = limit - len(_OMITTED)\n    # limit >= 32 -> remaining >= 32-15=17\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nMarker length: \"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15? \"<omitted>\" is 9 chars: < o m i t t e d > = 9. total 15. Budget 32 -> remaining 17 -> head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example.\n\nNow `_setup_logger` must pass max_message_chars from envs config. Import: logger.py importing deepinfer.environ — check for circular imports: environ.py imports? Let me check top of environ.py lines 1-30. Earlier read at lines 1-30 in this request's history? The recent tool results list read_range environ 1-30 but content not shown in evidence. I need to check imports of environ to ensure logger importing envs is safe and whether logger already imports envs.\n\nI haven't seen logger imports envs. I need to check environ.py imports (does it import logger? probably not). Let me read the first 30 lines of environ.py again? I have full read registered... but I don't have content in context. To be safe, do a read of environ lines 1-30 and search logger.py for \"environ\".\n\nActually the recent evidence shows environ lines 31-94 and 94-221; the head lines 1-30 were read in a tool call but content not delivered as evidence. Let me search for \"import\" in both files via search_code.\n\nPlan: one action: read environ 1-30 and search logger for \"envs\"/\"environ\". Then edit.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nAlso stats_logger appears to already call logger.info(m) in _log_worker without extra — need `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNote file hashes: environ fe2c..., logger 5b8f..., stats 9ef9... (all registered full reads, unchanged).\n\nLet's read heads."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10127,"completion_tokens":2234,"tota